 the description, our Turing machine starts in the following configuration:\n\n![](images/71/711875377cb3362858228b74d0deeebed554081de0c62a139af643e466cf9a42.png)\n\n- The machine observes symbol $0$ in state $q_1$. According to the transition table,\n$\\delta(q_1,0) = (q_1,1,R)$.\nSo, we should stay in state $q_1$, write $1$ into the cell, and move right. Performing this transition gives us the following new configuration:\n\n![](images/1a/1a4f49e67cccaf32a9dd96270e0d61e135bad0a01bd43ecc131b99cf858436d4.png)\n\nFinally, the machine observes the symbol $1$ in state $q_1$. According to the transition table, the transition function is not defined at $(q_1,1)$. So, the machine gets stuck." 13194139533375 17592186045585 true] [17592186203779 1069 "e-15701" 13194139533375 17592186045585 true] [17592186203779 1071 "![](images/1e/1e14a553cb905fcf3771a41a4f9f8a869bedab9c5f3fda1c8a51bcfdf99d980f.png)\n\nThe string $101$ is written on the tape of the Turing machine given by the transition table above, where $\\square$ is the blank symbol. The tape head initially observes the left-most symbol of the string in state $q_1$. Write the sequence of the machine's configurations in terms of instantaneous descriptions." 13194139533375 17592186045585 true] [17592186203779 1072 #uuid "3f639c29-5137-5832-8d8b-770c649017a2" 13194139533375 17592186045585 true] [17592186203779 1074 "First, let's summarize the nontrivial commands from our transition table:\n\n| Command | Description |\n| --- | ---: |\n| $(q_1, 1) \\to (q_2, 1, R)$ | In state $q_1$, observes $1{:}$<br>swap to state $q_2$, write $1$, and move right. |\n| $(q_1, \\square) \\to (q_1, 1, L)$ | In state $q_1$, observes $\\square{:}$<br>stay in state $q_1$, write $1$, and move left. |\n| $(q_2, 0) \\to (q_2, 1, R)$ | In state $q_2$, observes $0{:}$<br>stay in state $q_2$, write $1$, and move right. |\n| $(q_2, 1) \\to (q_0, 1, R)$ | In state $q_2$, observes $1{:}$<br>stay in state $q_0$, write $1$, and move right. |\n\nThe machine goes through the following configurations:\n\n| Instantaneous Description | Tape Diagram |\n| --- | ---: |\n| $q_1 101$ | ![](images/48/4841788be8a5481ad4483facd6739d600fba5e179178485a8561a48f77f8b475.png) |\n| $1 q_201$ | ![](images/8d/8db8c14cdf958561bada60ffe40cf37c5cedf21ddaf824744dc0efb9965d80f1.png) |\n| $11 q_2 1$ | ![](images/ea/eadab4ef19c92c31b04e2bb0096d04e509b82f64eb6e5fa5b306519eb4fd9964.png) |\n| $111q_0 \\square$ | ![](images/9f/9f389f1149ffe4c2d3797d60aff14f2b80fe43d9d61cbd76e104dd18956eafa9.png) |\n\nUsing the $\\vdash$ notation, we get the following:\n\n$$\nq_{1}101 \\mid ⊢1q_{2}01; ⊢11q_{2}1; ⊢111q_{0}◻\n$$" 13194139533375 17592186045585 true] [17592186203780 1069 "e-15829" 13194139533375 17592186045585 true] [17592186203780 1071 "![](images/d5/d55dbc3181b4cfbfc340dd68153541eae13184774bea756dd361dd2446e808e3.png)\n\nIn the diagram above, the parallel lines $\\overset{\\longleftrightarrow}{AB}$ and $\\overset{\\longleftrightarrow}{CD}$ are crossed by the transversal $\\overset{\\longleftrightarrow}{EF}$.\n\nConsider the following statement:\n\n> $m\\angle BJE + m\\angle FID=180^\\circ$.\n\nThe table below gives the proof of the statement. Fill in the missing reasons and thus complete the proof.\n\n|  | Statement | Reason |\n| --- | ---: | ---: |\n| 1 | $\\angle{CIF}$ and $\\angle{FID}$ are supplementary |  |\n| 2 | $m\\angle{CIF} + m\\angle{FID} = 180^\\circ$ | Definition of supplementary angles |\n| 3 | $\\angle{CIF} \\cong \\angle{DIJ}$ | Vertical angles theorem |\n| 4 | $\\angle{DIJ} \\cong \\angle{BJE}$ |  |\n| 5 | $\\angle{CIF} \\cong \\angle{BJE}$ |  |\n| 6 | $m\\angle{CIF} = m\\angle{BJE}$ | Definition of congruent angles |\n| 7 | $m\\angle{BJE} + m\\angle{FID} = 180^{\\circ}$ | Substitution |" 13194139533375 17592186045585 true] [17592186203780 1072 #uuid "4ecf0363-4b46-5944-a6f6-eeada31f76cf" 13194139533375 17592186045585 true] [17592186203780 1074 "The correct proof is shown below.\n\n|  | Statement | Reason |\n| --- | ---: | ---: |\n| 1 | $\\angle{CIF}$ and $\\angle{FID}$ are supplementary | $\\bbox[3pt,Gainsboro]{\\color{blue}\\textrm{Linear pair postulate}}$ |\n| 2 | $m\\angle{CIF} + m\\angle{FID} = 180^\\circ$ | Definition of supplementary angles |\n| 3 | $\\angle{CIF} \\cong \\angle{DIJ}$ | Vertical angles theorem |\n| 4 | $\\angle{DIJ} \\cong \\angle{BJE}$ | $\\bbox[3pt,Gainsboro]{\\color{blue}\\textrm{Corresponding angles postulate}}$ |\n| 5 | $\\angle{CIF} \\cong \\angle{BJE}$ | $\\bbox[3pt,Gainsboro]{\\color{blue}\\textrm{Transitive property of congruence}}$ |\n| 6 | $m\\angle{CIF} = m\\angle{BJE}$ | Definition of congruent angles |\n| 7 | $m\\angle{BJE} + m\\angle{FID} = 180^{\\circ}$ | Substitution |\n\nLet's examine the rows of the table with missing parts in turn.\n\n- Consider row 1. Note that $\\angle{CIF}$ and $\\angle{FID}$ form a linear pair. Therefore, by the linear pair postulate, these angles are supplementary.\n- Consider row 4. Note that $\\angle DIJ$ and $\\angle BJE$ are corresponding angles. Therefore, by the corresponding angles postulate, these angles are congruent.\n- Consider row 5. From rows 3 and 4, we have\n${\\color{blue}{\\angle CIF}} \\cong \\angle DIJ\\quad\\textrm{and}\\quad \\angle DIJ \\cong {\\color{blue}{\\angle{BJE}}}$.\nTherefore, by the transitive property of congruence, we have\n${\\color{blue}{\\angle CIF}} \\cong {\\color{blue}{\\angle BJE}}$." 13194139533375 17592186045585 true] [17592186203781 1069 "e-15831" 13194139533375 17592186045585 true] [17592186203781 1071 "The lines $r$ and $s$ are perpendicular. At the point of intersection, they form a linear pair of angles $\\angle a$ and $\\angle b$.\n\nConsider the following statement:\n\n> $\\angle a \\cong \\angle b$\n\nThe table below provides proof of this statement. Please fill in the missing reasons to complete the proof.\n\n|  | Statement | Reason |\n| --- | ---: | ---: |\n| 1 | $r \\perp s$ | Given |\n| 2 | $m\\angle a= 90^\\circ$ | Definition of perpendicular lines |\n| 3 | $\\angle a$ and $\\angle b$ are supplementary |  |\n| 4 | $m\\angle a+ m\\angle b=180^\\circ$ | Definition of supplementary angles |\n| 5 | $90^\\circ + m\\angle b = 180^\\circ$ | Substitution |\n| 6 | $m\\angle b = 90^\\circ$ |  |\n| 7 | $90^\\circ = m\\angle b$ | Symmetric property of equality |\n| 8 | $m\\angle a = m\\angle b$ | Transitive property of equality |\n| 9 | $\\angle a \\cong \\angle b$ |  |" 13194139533375 17592186045585 true] [17592186203781 1072 #uuid "140e5780-29ae-5fb4-94f2-3e8428b9700a" 13194139533375 17592186045585 true] [17592186203781 1074 "![](images/de/def8939d5b6a390496bbf83b2b5d595980c27189c4cbfea3af1e492dba4688b9.png)\n\nA sketch of the given information is shown above, and the correct proof is below.\n\n|  | Statement | Reason |\n| --- | ---: | ---: |\n| 1 | $r \\perp s$ | Given |\n| 2 | $m\\angle a= 90^\\circ$ | Definition of perpendicular lines |\n| 3 | $\\angle a$ and $\\angle b$ are supplementary | $\\bbox[3pt,Gainsboro]{\\color{blue}\\textrm{Linear pair postulate}}$ |\n| 4 | $m\\angle a+ m\\angle b=180^\\circ$ | Definition of supplementary angles |\n| 5 | $90^\\circ + m\\angle b = 180^\\circ$ | Substitution |\n| 6 | $m\\angle b = 90^\\circ$ | $\\bbox[3pt,Gainsboro]{\\color{blue}\\textrm{Addition principle}}$ |\n| 7 | $90^\\circ = m\\angle b$ | Symmetric property of equality |\n| 8 | $m\\angle a = m\\angle b$ | Transitive property of equality |\n| 9 | $\\angle a \\cong \\angle b$ | $\\bbox[3pt,Gainsboro]{\\color{blue}\\textrm{Definition of congruent angles}}$ |\n\nLet's examine the rows of the table with missing parts in turn.\n\n- Consider row 3. Notice that $\\angle a$ and $\\angle b$ form a linear pair. Therefore, by the linear pair postulate, these angles are supplementary.\n- Consider row 6. From row 5, we have that $90^\\circ + m\\angle b = 180^\\circ$. By the addition principle, we can subtract $90^\\circ$ from both sides of this equation:\n$90^{∘} + m∠b|= 180^{∘}; 90^{∘} + m∠b - 90^{∘}|= 180^{∘} - 90^{∘}; 90^{∘} + m∠b - 90^{∘}|= 90^{∘}; m∠b|= 90^{∘}$\n- Consider row 9. From row 8, $\\angle a$ and $\\angle b$ have the same measures. Therefore, by definition of congruent angles, these angles are congruent." 13194139533375 17592186045585 true] [17592186203782 1069 "e-15836" 13194139533375 17592186045585 true] [17592186203782 1071 "![](images/9f/9f7d9bbcb2de8fccc1c947b4daad7ba543e2a276fd545eebe4b41de5952d9b10.png)\n\nIn the diagram above, the parallel lines $l,m$, and $n$ are crossed by the transversal $t$.\n\nConsider the following statement:\n\n> $m\\angle AIJ = \\gamma$\n\nThe table below provides proof of the statement. Fill in the missing reasons to complete the proof.\n\n|  | Statement | Reason |\n| --- | ---: | ---: |\n| 1 | $\\angle ABC \\cong \\angle DBE$ |  |\n| 2 | $\\angle AIJ \\cong \\angle ABC$ |  |\n| 3 | $\\angle AIJ \\cong \\angle DBE$ |  |\n| 4 | $m\\angle AIJ = \\gamma$ |  |" 13194139533375 17592186045585 true] [17592186203782 1072 #uuid "e748b9d8-67e5-55c5-971a-bbda7dc74755" 13194139533375 17592186045585 true] [17592186203782 1074 "The correct proof is shown below.\n\n|  | Statement | Reason |\n| --- | ---: | ---: |\n| 1 | $\\angle ABC \\cong \\angle DBE$ | $\\bbox[3pt,Gainsboro]{\\color{blue}\\textrm{Vertical angles theorem}}$ |\n| 2 | $\\angle AIJ \\cong \\angle ABC$ | $\\bbox[3pt,Gainsboro]{\\color{blue}\\textrm{Corresponding angles postulate}}$ |\n| 3 | $\\angle AIJ \\cong \\angle DBE$ | $\\bbox[3pt,Gainsboro]{\\color{blue}\\textrm{Transitive property of congruence}}$ |\n| 4 | $m\\angle AIJ = \\gamma$ | $\\bbox[3pt,Gainsboro]{\\color{blue}\\textrm{Definition of congruent angles}}$ |\n\nLet's examine each row in turn.\n\n1. Notice from the diagram that $\\angle ABC$ and $\\angle DBE$ are vertical angles. The vertical angles theorem states that such angles must be congruent:\n$\\angle ABC \\cong \\angle DBE$\n2. Notice from the diagram that $\\angle AIJ$ and $\\angle ABC$ are corresponding angles. The corresponding angles postulate states that such angles must be congruent:\n$\\angle AIJ \\cong \\angle ABC$\n3. From rows 1 and 2, we have\n${\\color{blue}\\angle AIJ} \\cong \\angle ABC \\quad\\text{and}\\quad \\angle ABC \\cong {\\color{blue}\\angle DBE}$.\nTherefore, by the transitive property of congruence, we have\n${\\color{blue}\\angle AIJ} \\cong {\\color{blue}\\angle DBE}$.\n4. From row 3, we have that $\\angle AIJ \\cong \\angle DBE$. Therefore, by the definition of congruent angles, these angles have equal measure:\n$m\\angle AIJ = m\\angle DBE$\nFinally, since $m\\angle DBE = \\gamma$, we have\n$m\\angle AIJ = \\gamma$." 13194139533375 17592186045585 true] [17592186203783 1069 "e-15837" 13194139533375 17592186045585 true] [17592186203783 1071 "Suppose $\\overset{\\longleftrightarrow}{CD}$ is the perpendicular bisector of $\\overline{AB}$, and $C$ is a point on $\\overline{AB}$.\n\nConsider the following statement:\n\n> $\\overline{AD} \\cong \\overline{DB}$\n\nThe table below provides proof of this statement. Complete the proof by filling in the missing entries.\n\n|  | Statement | Reason |\n| --- | ---: | ---: |\n| 1 | $m\\angle ACD = 90^\\circ$ | Definition of perpendicular lines |\n| 2 | $AD^2 = AC^2 + DC^2$ |  |\n| 3 | $m\\angle DCB = 90^\\circ$ | Definition of perpendicular lines |\n| 4 | $DB^2 = CB^2 + DC^2$ | Pythagorean theorem for $\\triangle DCB$ |\n| 5 | $\\overline{CB} \\cong \\overline{AC}$ |  |\n| 6 | $CB = AC$ | Definition of congruent segments |\n| 7 | $DB^2 = AC^2 + DC^2$ |  |\n| 8 | $AC^2 + DC^2 = DB^2$ | Symmetric property of equality |\n| 9 | $AD^2 = DB^2$ |  |\n| 10 | $AD = DB$ | Algebra |" 13194139533375 17592186045585 true] [17592186203783 1072 #uuid "8e2b994c-0044-53f0-9a5c-4f250a847b19" 13194139533375 17592186045585 true] [17592186203783 1074 "![](images/2b/2b6d992c05a2e3286d5c8a7fc57b8d59e67f2faac291817d22ef5ba515877b6b.png)\n\nA sketch of the given information is shown above, and the correct proof is below.\n\n|  | Statement | Reason |\n| --- | ---: | ---: |\n| 1 | $m\\angle ACD = 90^\\circ$ | Definition of perpendicular lines |\n| 2 | $AD^2 = AC^2 + DC^2$ | $\\bbox[3pt,Gainsboro]{\\color{blue}\\textrm{Pythagorean theorem for}\\triangle ACD}$ |\n| 3 | $m\\angle DCB = 90^\\circ$ | Definition of perpendicular lines |\n| 4 | $DB^2 = CB^2 + DC^2$ | Pythagorean theorem for $\\triangle DCB$ |\n| 5 | $\\overline{CB} \\cong \\overline{AC}$ | $\\bbox[3pt,Gainsboro]{\\color{blue}\\textrm{Definition of bisector}}$ |\n| 6 | $CB = AC$ | Definition of congruent segments |\n| 7 | $DB^2 = AC^2 + DC^2$ | $\\bbox[3pt,Gainsboro]{\\color{blue}\\textrm{Substitution}}$ |\n| 8 | $AC^2 + DC^2 = DB^2$ | Symmetric property of equality |\n| 9 | $AD^2 = DB^2$ | $\\bbox[3pt,Gainsboro]{\\color{blue}\\textrm{Transitive property of equality}}$ |\n| 10 | $AD = DB$ | Algebra |\n\nLet's examine the rows of the table with missing parts in turn.\n\n- Consider row 2. From row 1, we have that $m\\angle ACD = 90^\\circ$. Then, by the Pythagorean theorem applied to $\\triangle ACD$, we have\n$AD^2 = AC^2 + DC^2$.\n- Consider row 5. From the given information, $\\overset{\\longleftrightarrow}{CD}$ is the perpendicular bisector of $\\overline{AB}$ and $C$ lies on $\\overline{AB}$. By the definition of a bisector, we have\n$\\overline{CB} \\cong \\overline{AC}$.\n- Consider row 7. From rows 6 and 4, we have that $CB = AC$ and $DB^2 = CB^2 + DC^2$. Therefore, by substituting the first into the second,\n$DB^2 = CB^2 + DC^2 = AC^2 + DC^2$.\n- Consider row 9. From rows 2 and 8, we have\n${\\color{blue}AD^2} = AC^2 + DC^2 \\quad\\text{and}\\quad AC^2 + DC^2 = {\\color{blue}DB^2}$.\nTherefore, by the transitive property of congruence, we have\n${\\color{blue}AD^2} = {\\color{blue}DB^2}$." 13194139533375 17592186045585 true] [17592186203784 1069 "e-15838" 13194139533375 17592186045585 true] [17592186203784 1071 "![](images/96/96fc08b643a86a53f86dd35756ec9c71317e53d138d3c1c4d93b1dcced1512ac.png)\n\nIn the diagram above, $\\overset{\\longleftrightarrow}{CD}$ is the perpendicular bisector of $\\overline{AB}$.\n\nConsider the following statement:\n\n> *The segments $\\overline{DB}$ and $\\overline{DA}$ are congruent.*\n\nThe table below provides proof of this statement. Fill in the missing reasons and thus complete the proof.\n\n|  | Statement | Reason |\n| --- | ---: | ---: |\n| 1 | $m\\angle DCB = 90^\\circ$ |  |\n| 2 | $\\angle DCB$ and $\\angle DCA$ are supplementary |  |\n| 3 | $m\\angle DCB +m\\angle DCA = 180^\\circ$ |  |\n| 4 | $m\\angle DCA =180^\\circ - m\\angle DCB$ |  |\n| 5 | $m\\angle DCA = 90^\\circ$ |  |\n| 6 | $m\\angle DCB = m\\angle DCA$ |  |\n| 7 | $\\angle DCB \\cong \\angle DCA$ |  |\n| 8 | $\\overline{AC} \\cong \\overline{CB}$ |  |\n| 9 | $\\overline{CD} \\cong \\overline{CD}$ |  |\n| 10 | $\\triangle{DCB} \\cong \\triangle{DCA}$ |  |\n| 11 | $\\overline{DB} \\cong \\overline{DA}$ |  |" 13194139533375 17592186045585 true] [17592186203784 1072 #uuid "4cf038ff-bd8f-5f82-80c7-04e0a0ddd4c1" 13194139533375 17592186045585 true] [17592186203784 1074 "The correct proof is below.\n\n|  | Statement | Reason |\n| --- | ---: | ---: |\n| 1 | $m\\angle DCB = 90^\\circ$ | $\\bbox[3pt,Gainsboro]{\\color{blue}\\textrm{Given}}$ |\n| 2 | $\\angle DCB$ and $\\angle DCA$ are supplementary | $\\bbox[3pt,Gainsboro]{\\color{blue}\\textrm{Linear pair postulate}}$ |\n| 3 | $m\\angle DCB +m\\angle DCA = 180^\\circ$ | $\\bbox[3pt,Gainsboro]{\\color{blue}\\textrm{Definition of supplementary angles}}$ |\n| 4 | $m\\angle DCA =180^\\circ - m\\angle DCB$ | $\\bbox[3pt,Gainsboro]{\\color{blue}\\textrm{Addition principle}}$ |\n| 5 | $m\\angle DCA = 90^\\circ$ | $\\bbox[3pt,Gainsboro]{\\color{blue}\\textrm{Substitution}}$ |\n| 6 | $m\\angle DCB = m\\angle DCA$ | $\\bbox[3pt,Gainsboro]{\\color{blue}\\textrm{Transitive property of equality}}$ |\n| 7 | $\\angle DCB \\cong \\angle DCA$ | $\\bbox[3pt,Gainsboro]{\\color{blue}\\textrm{Definition of congruent angles}}$ |\n| 8 | $\\overline{AC} \\cong \\overline{CB}$ | $\\bbox[3pt,Gainsboro]{\\color{blue}\\textrm{Definition of perpendicular bisector}}$ |\n| 9 | $\\overline{CD} \\cong \\overline{CD}$ | $\\bbox[3pt,Gainsboro]{\\color{blue}\\textrm{Reflexive property of congruence}}$ |\n| 10 | $\\triangle{DCB} \\cong \\triangle{DCA}$ | $\\bbox[3pt,Gainsboro]{\\color{blue}\\textrm{SAS criterion}}$ |\n| 11 | $\\overline{DB} \\cong \\overline{DA}$ | $\\bbox[3pt,Gainsboro]{\\color{blue}\\textrm{CPCTC}}$ |\n\nLet's examine each row in turn.\n\n1. We're given that $\\overset{\\longleftrightarrow}{CD}$ is the perpendicular bisector of $\\overline{AB}$. Then, by the definition of perpendicular lines, the angles formed by the intersection of $\\overset{\\longleftrightarrow}{CD}$ and $\\overline{AB}$ have a measure of $90^\\circ$. So, we have that $m\\angle{DCB} = 90^\\circ$.\n2. Notice that $\\angle{DCB}$ and $\\angle{DCA}$ form a linear pair. Therefore, these angles are supplementary by the linear pair postulate.\n3. From row 2, $\\angle{DCB}$ and $\\angle{DCA}$ are supplementary. Therefore, $m\\angle DCB +m\\angle DCA = 180^\\circ$ by the definition of supplementary angles.\n4. From row 3, $m\\angle DCB +m\\angle DCA = 180^\\circ$. By the addition principle, we can subtract $m\\angle{DCB}$ from both sides of this equation:\n$m∠DCB + m∠DCA|= 180^{∘}; m∠DCB + m∠DCA - m∠DCB|= 180^{∘} - m∠DCB; m∠DCB + m∠DCA - m∠DCB|= 180^{∘} - m∠DCB; m∠DCA|= 180^{∘} - m∠DCB$\n5. From rows 1 and 4, $m\\angle DCB = 90^\\circ$ and $m\\angle DCA =180^\\circ - m\\angle DCB$. Therefore, by substituting the first into the second,\n$m\\angle DCA %= 180^\\circ - m\\angle DCB = 180^\\circ - 90^\\circ = 90^\\circ$.\n6. From rows 1 and 5, we have $m\\angle DCB = 90^\\circ = m\\angle DCA$. Therefore, by the transitive property of equality,\n$m\\angle{DCB} = m\\angle{DCA}$.\n7. From row 6, $\\angle{DCB}$ and $m\\angle{DCA}$ have the same measure. Therefore, these angles are congruent by the definition of congruent angles.\n8. From the given information, $\\overset{\\longleftrightarrow}{CD}$ is the perpendicular bisector of $\\overline{AB}$ and $C$ lies on $\\overline{AB}$. By the definition of a perpendicular bisector, we have\n$\\overline{AC} \\cong \\overline{CB}$.\n9. The reflexive property of congruence states that every segment is congruent to itself. Then, $\\overline{CD} \\cong \\overline{CD}$ by the reflexive property of congruence.\n10. From rows 8, 7, and 9, we have\n$\\overline{AC} \\cong \\overline{CB}, \\qquad \\angle DCB \\cong \\angle DCA, \\qquad \\overline{CD} \\cong \\overline{CD}$.\nTherefore, by the SAS criterion, $\\triangle{DCB} \\cong \\triangle{DCA}$.\n11. From row 10, $\\triangle{{\\color{red}D}C{\\color{red}B}} \\cong \\triangle{{\\color{red}D}C{\\color{red}A}}$. Then, $\\overline{DB} \\cong \\overline{DA}$ by CPCTC." 13194139533375 17592186045585 true] [17592186203785 1069 "e-15842" 13194139533375 17592186045585 true] [17592186203785 1071 "![](images/7b/7b84e3bb61b53736bc4f73911c121354c1b7d2054d47ecb6ce4c48e2bf715992.png)\n\nGiven that $m\\angle EJB+m\\angle HKF =180^\\circ$, by which theorem does it follow that $\\overleftrightarrow{AB}$ and $\\overleftrightarrow{GH}$ are parallel?" 13194139533375 17592186045585 true] [17592186203785 1072 #uuid "b55de9fa-5f71-5539-a943-f64aab904da6" 13194139533375 17592186045585 true] [17592186203785 1074 "If a transversal cuts two lines, we get two pairs of consecutive exterior angles.\n\n![](images/33/3369d1c88954468f913910930ec211929f442c131b6ebc2a60f73fd219710ce9.png)\n\nSo, in the given diagram, $\\angle EJB$ and $\\angle HKF$ are consecutive exterior angles.\n\nThe **converse consecutive exterior angles theorem** states that if two lines are cut by a transversal so that the consecutive exterior angles are supplementary, then the lines are parallel.\n\nWe are given that $\\angle EJB$ and $\\angle HKF$ are supplementary. Therefore, $\\overleftrightarrow{AB}\\parallel \\overleftrightarrow{GH}$ by the converse consecutive exterior angles theorem." 13194139533375 17592186045585 true] [17592186203786 1069 "e-15843" 13194139533375 17592186045585 true] [17592186203786 1071 "![](images/24/245b528b754ea759e5c8e41a7a795ff636f8a5ce375f537670354d91bbfa5a1c.png)\n\nIn the diagram above, the parallel lines $\\overset{\\longleftrightarrow}{AB}$ and $\\overset{\\longleftrightarrow}{CD}$ are crossed by the transversal $\\overset{\\longleftrightarrow}{EF}$.\n\nConsider the following statement:\n\n> $\\angle{BJI}$ and $\\angle{DIJ}$ are supplementary.\n\nThe table below provides proof of the statement. Complete the proof by filling in the missing entries.\n\n|  | Statement | Reason |\n| --- | ---: | ---: |\n| 1 | $\\angle{AJF} \\cong \\angle{BJI}$ |  |\n| 2 | $\\angle{CIJ} \\cong \\angle{AJF}$ |  |\n| 3 | $\\angle{CIJ} \\cong \\angle{BJI}$ |  |\n| 4 | $m\\angle{CIJ} = m\\angle{BJI}$ |  |\n| 5 | $\\angle{CIJ}$ and $\\angle{DIJ}$ are supplementary |  |\n| 6 | $m\\angle{CIJ} + m\\angle{DIJ} = 180^\\circ$ |  |\n| 7 | $m\\angle{BJI} + m\\angle{DIJ} = 180^\\circ$ |  |\n| 8 | $\\angle{BJI}$ and $\\angle{DIJ}$ are supplementary |  |" 13194139533375 17592186045585 true] [17592186203786 1072 #uuid "a2e316e6-7583-5b66-ac63-09809526104b" 13194139533375 17592186045585 true] [17592186203786 1074 "The correct proof is shown below.\n\n|  | Statement | Reason |\n| --- | ---: | ---: |\n| 1 | $\\angle{AJF} \\cong \\angle{BJI}$ | $\\bbox[3pt,Gainsboro]{\\color{blue}\\textrm{Vertical angles theorem}}$ |\n| 2 | $\\angle{CIJ} \\cong \\angle{AJF}$ | $\\bbox[3pt,Gainsboro]{\\color{blue}\\textrm{Corresponding angles postulate}}$ |\n| 3 | $\\angle{CIJ} \\cong \\angle{BJI}$ | $\\bbox[3pt,Gainsboro]{\\color{blue}\\textrm{Transitive property of congruence}}$ |\n| 4 | $m\\angle{CIJ} = m\\angle{BJI}$ | $\\bbox[3pt,Gainsboro]{\\color{blue}\\textrm{Definition of congruent angles}}$ |\n| 5 | $\\angle{CIJ}$ and $\\angle{DIJ}$ are supplementary | $\\bbox[3pt,Gainsboro]{\\color{blue}\\textrm{Linear pair postulate}}$ |\n| 6 | $m\\angle{CIJ} + m\\angle{DIJ} = 180^\\circ$ | $\\bbox[3pt,Gainsboro]{\\color{blue}\\textrm{Definition of supplementary angles}}$ |\n| 7 | $m\\angle{BJI} + m\\angle{DIJ} = 180^\\circ$ | $\\bbox[3pt,Gainsboro]{\\color{blue}\\textrm{Substitution}}$ |\n| 8 | $\\angle{BJI}$ and $\\angle{DIJ}$ are supplementary | $\\bbox[3pt,Gainsboro]{\\color{blue}\\textrm{Definition of supplementary angles}}$ |\n\nLet's examine each row in turn.\n\n1. Note that $\\angle AJF$ and $\\angle BJI$ are vertical angles. Therefore, these angles are congruent by the vertical angles theorem.\n2. Note that $\\angle {CIJ}$ and $\\angle{AJF}$ are corresponding angles. Therefore, these angles are congruent by the corresponding angles postulate.\n3. From rows 2 and 1, we have\n${\\color{blue}{\\angle{CIJ}}} \\cong \\angle{AJF}\\quad\\textrm{and}\\quad\\angle{AJF} \\cong {\\color{blue}{\\angle{BJI}}}$.\nTherefore, by the transitive property of congruence, we have\n${\\color{blue}{\\angle{CIJ}}} \\cong {\\color{blue}{\\angle{BJI}}}$.\n4. From row 3, we have $\\angle{CIJ} \\cong \\angle{BJI}$. Therefore, we have that $m\\angle{CIJ} = m\\angle{BJI}$ by the definition of congruent angles.\n5. Note that $\\angle {CIJ}$ and $\\angle {DIJ}$ form a linear pair. Therefore, these angles are supplementary by the linear pair postulate.\n6. From row 5, we have that $\\angle {CIJ}$ and $\\angle {BJI}$ are supplementary angles. Therefore, by the definition of supplementary angles, $m\\angle{CIJ} + m\\angle{DIJ} = 180^\\circ$.\n7. From row 6, we have\n${\\color{blue}{m\\angle{CIJ}}} + m\\angle{DIJ} = 180^\\circ$.\nFrom row 4, we have ${\\color{blue}{m\\angle{CIJ}}} = m\\angle{BJI}$. Substituting this into the above, we have\n$m\\angle{BJI} + m\\angle{DIJ} = 180^\\circ$.\n8. From row 7, we have that $m\\angle{BJI} + m\\angle{DIJ} = 180^\\circ$. Therefore, these angles are supplementary by the definition of supplementary angles." 13194139533375 17592186045585 true] [17592186203787 1069 "e-15844" 13194139533375 17592186045585 true] [17592186203787 1071 "![](images/7a/7a1491076a92059ccba16a57b50ef31d25fc203b9c0a4001895816125142b71f.png)\n\nIn the diagram above, the lines $\\overleftrightarrow{AB}$ and $\\overleftrightarrow{CD}$ are crossed by the transversal $\\overleftrightarrow{EF}$ and $\\angle EGB \\cong \\angle CHF$.\n\nConsider the following statement:\n\n> *$\\overleftrightarrow{AB}$ and $\\overleftrightarrow{CD}$ are parallel.*\n\nThe table below gives the proof of the statement. Fill in the missing entries in the table and thus complete the proof.\n\n|  | Statement | Reason |\n| --- | ---: | ---: |\n| 1 | $\\angle CHF$ and $\\angle FHD$ are supplementary |  |\n| 2 | $m\\angle CHF + m\\angle FHD = 180^\\circ$ | Definition of supplementary angles |\n| 3 | $m\\angle CHF = 180^\\circ - m\\angle FHD$ |  |\n| 4 | $\\angle EGB$ and $\\angle BGH$ are supplementary | Linear pair postulate |\n| 5 | $m\\angle EGB + m\\angle BGH = 180^\\circ$ | Definition of supplementary angles |\n| 6 | $\\angle EGB \\cong \\angle CHF$ | Given |\n| 7 | $m\\angle EGB = 180^\\circ - m\\angle FHD$ |  |\n| 8 | $(180^\\circ - m\\angle FHD) + m\\angle BGH = 180^\\circ$ | Substitution |\n| 9 | $m\\angle BGH = m\\angle FHD$ | Addition principle |\n| 10 | $\\angle BGH \\cong \\angle FHD$ | Definition of congruent angles |\n| 11 | $\\overleftrightarrow{AB}$ and $\\overleftrightarrow{CD}$ are parallel |  |" 13194139533375 17592186045585 true] [17592186203787 1072 #uuid "5048f941-5384-55c7-a919-5d2579307ffd" 13194139533375 17592186045585 true] [17592186203787 1074 "The correct proof is shown below.\n\n|  | Statement | Reason |\n| --- | ---: | ---: |\n| 1 | $\\angle CHF$ and $\\angle FHD$ are supplementary | $\\bbox[3pt,Gainsboro]{\\color{blue}\\textrm{Linear pair postulate}}$ |\n| 2 | $m\\angle CHF + m\\angle FHD = 180^\\circ$ | Definition of supplementary angles |\n| 3 | $m\\angle CHF = 180^\\circ - m\\angle FHD$ | $\\bbox[3pt,Gainsboro]{\\color{blue}\\textrm{Addition principle}}$ |\n| 4 | $\\angle EGB$ and $\\angle BGH$ are supplementary | Linear pair postulate |\n| 5 | $m\\angle EGB + m\\angle BGH = 180^\\circ$ | Definition of supplementary angles |\n| 6 | $\\angle EGB \\cong \\angle CHF$ | Given |\n| 7 | $m\\angle EGB = 180^\\circ - m\\angle FHD$ | $\\bbox[3pt,Gainsboro]{\\color{blue}\\textrm{Definition of congruent angles}}$ |\n| 8 | $(180^\\circ - m\\angle FHD) + m\\angle BGH = 180^\\circ$ | Substitution |\n| 9 | $m\\angle BGH = m\\angle FHD$ | Addition principle |\n| 10 | $\\angle BGH \\cong \\angle FHD$ | Definition of congruent angles |\n| 11 | $\\overleftrightarrow{AB}$ and $\\overleftrightarrow{CD}$ are parallel | $\\bbox[3pt,Gainsboro]{\\color{blue}\\textrm{Converse corresponding angles postulate}}$ |\n\nLet's examine the rows of the table with missing parts in turn.\n\n- Consider row 1. Notice from the diagram that $\\angle CHF$ and $\\angle FHD$ form a linear pair. The linear pair postulate states that such angles must be supplementary. Therefore, $\\angle CHF$ and $\\angle FHD$ are supplementary angles.\n- Consider row 3. From row 2, we have $m\\angle CHF + m\\angle FHD = 180^\\circ$. By the addition principle, we can subtract $m\\angle FHD$ from both sides\n$m∠CHF + m∠FHD|= 180^{∘}; m∠CHF + m∠FHD - m∠FHD|= 180^{∘} - m∠FHD; m∠CHF + m∠FHD - m∠FHD|= 180^{∘} - m∠FHD; m∠CHF|= 180^{∘} - m∠FHD$.\n- Consider row 7. From row 3, we have that $m\\angle CHF = 180^\\circ - m\\angle FHD$, and from row 6, we have that $\\angle EGB \\cong \\angle CHF$. Therefore, by the definition of congruent angles, these angles have equal measure:\n$m \\angle EGB= 180^\\circ - m\\angle FHD$\n- Consider row 11. From row 10, we have that $\\angle BGH\\cong \\angle FHD$. By the converse corresponding angles postulate, if two lines and a transversal form corresponding angles that are congruent, then the lines are parallel. Notice that $\\angle BGH$ and $\\angle FHD$ are corresponding angles formed by the lines $\\overleftrightarrow{AB}$ and $\\overleftrightarrow{CD}$ with the transversal $\\overleftrightarrow{EF}$. Therefore, $\\overleftrightarrow{AB}$ and $\\overleftrightarrow{CD}$ are parallel." 13194139533375 17592186045585 true] [1759218