92186204335 1069 "q-224345" 13194139533375 17592186045585 true] [17592186204335 1071 "Consider the following statement:\n\n> Let $x∼y$ if and only if $x + y$ is an even number for $x, y ∈ Z$. Then, $∼$ is a symmetric relation on $Z$.\n\nA proof of this statement is given below.\n\n> $L1$: Let $x, y ∈ Z$ and $x∼y$.\n> $L2$: Then, $x + y$ is an even number.\n> $L3$: So, $y + x = x + y$ is an even number.\n> $L4$: Thus, $y∼x$.\n> $L5$: Therefore, $∼$ is symmetric on $Z$.\n\nSelect the correct options in the following reasoning.\n\nLine $L2$ follows from line $L1$ by the definition of {{field-1}}.\n\nLine $L5$ follows from $L1$ and $L4$ by the {{field-2}} of {{field-3}}.\n\nTo prove that $∼$ is an equivalence relation, we also need to demonstrate that $∼$ is {{field-4}} and {{field-5}}." 13194139533375 17592186045585 true] [17592186204335 1072 #uuid "3079871d-db31-5e74-b187-ca8fd22b2b10" 13194139533375 17592186045585 true] [17592186204336 1068 #edb/ref 17592186200845 13194139533375 17592186045585 true] [17592186204336 1069 "q-224346" 13194139533375 17592186045585 true] [17592186204336 1071 "![](images/f9/f9448614876f83aff8a755a95dcd76323efefc50b516b2c9142e29d57c30f203.png)\n\nThe diameters of balls for bearings produced by machines X and Y are known to be normally distributed with equal variance. Engineers want to verify the claim that the average diameter of balls produced by machine X is not equal to the average diameter of balls produced by machine Y.\n\nThey independently and randomly sampled $n_{1} = 22$ balls produced by machine X and $n_{2} = 20$ balls produced by machine Y, finding that their mean diameters are $x^(―)_{1} = 20.09$ millimeters and $x^(―)_{2} = 19.85$ millimeters, respectively, and the pooled variance is $s_{p}^{2} = 0.1^{2}$. Which of the following statements are true?\n\n1. The end-points of the $96%$ confidence interval for the difference between the average diameters are approximately $0.24 ± 0.065$.\n2. The $96%$ confidence interval does not contain $0$.\n3. The confidence interval data suggests the mean diameter of balls produced by machine X is not equal to the mean diameter of balls produced by machine Y.\n\n*Hint: Use the fact that $P(T > 2.12) = 0.02$, where $T$ has a student's $t$-distribution with $40$ degrees of freedom, as shown in the diagram above.*\n\n> A scientific calculator is required to answer this question." 13194139533375 17592186045585 true] [17592186204336 1072 #uuid "bc9338c1-cb81-5239-8882-74da0e832df7" 13194139533375 17592186045585 true] [17592186204336 1073 true 13194139533375 17592186045585 true] [17592186204337 1068 #edb/ref 17592186200851 13194139533375 17592186045585 true] [17592186204337 1068 #edb/ref 17592186200854 13194139533375 17592186045585 true] [17592186204337 1068 #edb/ref 17592186200858 13194139533375 17592186045585 true] [17592186204337 1068 #edb/ref 17592186200862 13194139533375 17592186045585 true] [17592186204337 1068 #edb/ref 17592186200865 13194139533375 17592186045585 true] [17592186204337 1069 "q-224434" 13194139533375 17592186045585 true] [17592186204337 1071 "Prove the following theorem:\n\n> Let $a$ and $b$ be integers and $n$ be a positive integer. If $gcd(a, n) = k > 1$ with $k∣b$, then the congruence $ax≡b(modn)$ has exactly $k$ distinct solutions modulo $n$.\n\nFirst, we assume by hypothesis that $gcd(a, n) = k > 1$ and $k∣b$, meaning that there exist $a_{1}, n_{1}, b_{1} ∈ Z$ such that\n$n$ {{field-1}} {{field-2}} {{field-3}}\n\nSince $k$ is the {{field-4}} of $a$ and $n$, the integers $a_{1}$ and $n_{1}$ must satisfy {{field-5}}." 13194139533375 17592186045585 true] [17592186204337 1072 #uuid "e0e3d11b-0f69-5040-8e83-09ada18020df" 13194139533375 17592186045585 true] [17592186204338 1068 #edb/ref 17592186200869 13194139533375 17592186045585 true] [17592186204338 1068 #edb/ref 17592186200873 13194139533375 17592186045585 true] [17592186204338 1068 #edb/ref 17592186200877 13194139533375 17592186045585 true] [17592186204338 1068 #edb/ref 17592186200881 13194139533375 17592186045585 true] [17592186204338 1068 #edb/ref 17592186200885 13194139533375 17592186045585 true] [17592186204338 1068 #edb/ref 17592186200889 13194139533375 17592186045585 true] [17592186204338 1069 "q-224516" 13194139533375 17592186045585 true] [17592186204338 1071 "Consider the following statement:\n\n> Let $x∼y$ if and only if $\\sqrt{3}(x - y)$ is rational number, for $x, y ∈ R$. Then, $∼$ is a transitive relation on $R$.\n\nA proof of this statement is given below.\n\n> $L1$: Let $x, y, z ∈ R$ such that $x∼y$ and $y∼z$.\n> $L2$: Then, $\\sqrt{3}(x - y)$ and $\\sqrt{3}(y - z)$ are rational numbers.\n> $L3$: So, $\\sqrt{3}(x - z) = \\sqrt{3}(x - y) + \\sqrt{3}(y - z)$ is a rational number.\n> $L4$: Thus, $x∼z$.\n> $L5$: Therefore, $∼$ is transitive on $R$.\n\nSelect the correct options in the following reasoning.\n\nLine $L2$ follows from line $L1$ by the definition of {{field-1}}.\n\nLine $L3$ follows from $L2$ by the fact that the {{field-2}} of two {{field-3}} is another {{field-4}}.\n\nTo prove that $∼$ is an equivalence relation, we also need to demonstrate that $∼$ is {{field-5}} and {{field-6}}." 13194139533375 17592186045585 true] [17592186204338 1072 #uuid "957de12d-8dea-5387-a8b5-d37a9c6896c5" 13194139533375 17592186045585 true] [17592186204339 1068 #edb/ref 17592186200893 13194139533375 17592186045585 true] [17592186204339 1069 "q-224570" 13194139533375 17592186045585 true] [17592186204339 1071 "Let $a∼b$ if and only if the individuals $a$ and $b$ share the same birth month.\nProve that $∼$ is an equivalence relation on the set of all people.\n\nA relation $∼$ is an equivalence relation if it is\n{{field-1}}." 13194139533375 17592186045585 true] [17592186204339 1072 #uuid "d8b79d69-d2eb-5838-b111-78ed6af545de" 13194139533375 17592186045585 true] [17592186204340 1068 #edb/ref 17592186200898 13194139533375 17592186045585 true] [17592186204340 1068 #edb/ref 17592186200901 13194139533375 17592186045585 true] [17592186204340 1068 #edb/ref 17592186200906 13194139533375 17592186045585 true] [17592186204340 1068 #edb/ref 17592186200910 13194139533375 17592186045585 true] [17592186204340 1068 #edb/ref 17592186200913 13194139533375 17592186045585 true] [17592186204340 1069 "q-224686" 13194139533375 17592186045585 true] [17592186204340 1071 "Consider the following statement:\n\n> Let $a, b$, and $n$ be integers. If $gcd(a, n) = k > 1$ and $k∤b$, then the linear congruence $ax≡b(modn)$ has no solution.\n\nA proof of this statement is given below.\n\n> $L1$: We are given that $gcd(a, n) = k > 1$ and $k∤b$.\n> $L2$: So, there exists $a_{1}, n_{1} ∈ Z$ such that $a = a_{1}k$ and $n = n_{1}k$.\n> $L3$: Assume for a contradiction that $ax≡b(modn)$ has a solution.\n> $L4$: Then, there exists $x_{1} ∈ Z$ such that $ax_{1}≡b(modn)$.\n> $L5$: So, there exists $c ∈ Z$ such that $ax_{1} - b = nc$.\n> $L6$: Hence, $ax_{1} - nc = b$.\n> $L7$: As a result, $k(a_{1}x_{1} - n_{1}c) = b$.\n> $L8$: This implies that $k∣b$, which is a contradiction.\n> $L9$: Therefore, the assumption that $ax≡b(modn)$ has a solution is false.\n\nSelect the correct options in the following reasoning.\n\nLine $L2$ follows from line $L1$ by the\n{{field-1}}\nof\n{{field-2}}.\n\nLine $L5$ is equivalent to line $L4$ by the {{field-3}}\nof\n{{field-4}}.\n\nThe given proof is a proof by {{field-5}}." 13194139533375 17592186045585 true] [17592186204340 1072 #uuid "04fe9a80-37b6-577d-8ba8-f978f8b1c1b6" 13194139533375 17592186045585 true] [17592186204341 1068 #edb/ref 17592186200919 13194139533375 17592186045585 true] [17592186204341 1068 #edb/ref 17592186200923 13194139533375 17592186045585 true] [17592186204341 1068 #edb/ref 17592186200928 13194139533375 17592186045585 true] [17592186204341 1068 #edb/ref 17592186200932 13194139533375 17592186045585 true] [17592186204341 1068 #edb/ref 17592186200935 13194139533375 17592186045585 true] [17592186204341 1069 "q-224893" 13194139533375 17592186045585 true] [17592186204341 1071 "Prove the following theorem:\n\n> Let $a$ and $b$ be integers and $n$ be a positive integer. If $gcd(a, n) = k > 1$ with $k∣b$, then the congruence $ax≡b(modn)$ has exactly $k$ distinct solutions modulo $n$.\n\nA proof of this statement is given below.\n\n> $L1$: We are given that $gcd(a, n) = k > 1$ and $k∣b$.\n> $L2$: Then, $∃a_{1}, n_{1} ∈ Z$, such that $a = a_{1}k, n = n_{1}k$.\n> $L3$: And, in turn, $∃b_{1} ∈ Z, b = b_{1}k$.\n> $L4$: Since $k = gcd(a, n)$, we have that $gcd(a_{1}, n_{1}) = 1$.\n> $L5$: Therefore, $∃u, v ∈ Z$ such that $a_{1}u + n_{1}v = 1$.\n> $L6$: We can rewrite this equality as $a_{1}(b_{1}u) + n_{1}(b_{1}v) = b_{1}$.\n> $L7$: So, we have that $a_{1}(b_{1}u) + n_{1}(b_{1}v)≡b_{1}(modn_{1})$.\n> $L8$: This implies that $a_{1}(b_{1}u)≡b_{1}(modn_{1})$.\n> $L9$: So, $x_{1} = b_{1}u$ is a solution of the congruence $a_{1}x≡b_{1}(modn_{1})$.\n> $L10$: Now, it is true that $a_{1}x_{i}≡b_{1}(modn_{1})$, for $x_{i} = x_{1} + (i - 1)n_{1}, 1 \\le i \\le k$.\n> $L11$: Therefore, $(ka_{1})x_{i}≡(kb_{1})(modkn_{1})$.\n> $L12$: Thus, $ax_{i}≡b(modn)$.\n> $L13$: This means that $x_{i}$ is solution for original congruence $ax≡b(modn)$ for each $1 \\le i \\le k$.\n> $L14$: Now, suppose that $i, j ∈ {1, …, k - 1}, i \\ne j$ and $x_{i}≡x_{j}(modn)$.\n> $L15$: Then, $x_{1} + (i - 1)n_{1}≡x_{1} + (j - 1)n_{1}(modn)$.\n> $L16$: So, $(i - 1)n_{1}≡(j - 1)n_{1}(modn_{1}k)$.\n> $L17$: And we get that $i≡j(modk)$.\n> $L18$: Therefore, $i = j$, which is a contradiction.\n> $L19$: This means that the solutions $x_{i}$ are distinct and mutually non-congruent modulo $n$.\n> $L20$: Finally, we conclude that the congruence has exactly $k$ distinct solutions modulo $n$.\n\nSelect the correct options in the following reasoning.\n\nLine $L3$ follows from {{field-1}} in line $L1$ by {{field-2}}.\n\nLine $L8$ follows from line {{field-3}} by the fact that {{field-4}}.\n\nLine $L17$ follows from line $L16$ by the {{field-5}}." 13194139533375 17592186045585 true] [17592186204341 1072 #uuid "29528569-21bd